ay until you get  over here n minus one plus two that's  also going to be n plus one that's also  going to be n plus one and then finally  you have n plus one right over here plus  n plus one so what's this whole sum  going to be well how many of these n  plus ones or do we have well we have n  of them for every term in each of these  sums so this is one two three count all  the way to n you have n of these terms  so you have n n plus ones so if you add  something to itself n times or view if  you have something n times right over  here this is really just this is exactly  equivalent to n times n plus one so two  times that sum two times the sum of all  of the positive integers up to including  n is going to be equal to n times n plus  one so if you divide both sides by two  we get an expression for the sum so the  sum of all of the positive integers up  to an including n is going to be equal  to n times n plus 1 over 2 so here is a  proof where we didn't have to use  induction it's really kind of a pure  algebraic proof
 

